{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 2,
  "simulation_id": "sim-03",
  "member_id": "pearce-mark",
  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B017",
        "laureates": [
          "Francis Halzen",
          "Albrecht Karle",
          "Shigeru Yoshida"
        ],
        "citation": "For establishing high-energy neutrino astronomy with IceCube"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B024",
    "B044"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "IceCube established a new observational window on the high-energy universe and made neutrino astronomy a durable field, with astrophysical sources now identified in multiple settings. The detector and its observations are mature enough for judgment. Halzen’s role in conceiving the detector and scientific program, alongside Karle’s and Yoshida’s detector and analysis contributions, offers a defensible attribution within a large collaboration.",
    "case_against": "Individual credit is difficult to assign in a collaboration of this scale, and the proposed three names may not capture every decisive contribution. The field is also still developing, so the full reach of this new astronomy is not yet known.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "You regard the discovery of high-energy astrophysical neutrinos as well established, while noting that the field’s future reach and attribution within the collaboration remain uncertain.",
        "response": "I agree that the established observations are sufficient to recognize the opening of this observational window; a prize need not wait for the astronomy to reach its full potential. The attribution concern remains real, so the committee should verify the distinct contributions of Halzen, Karle, and Yoshida before fixing the citation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "You argue that Nd-Fe-B magnets are a mature materials discovery with clear technological consequences, while calling for careful review of independent development and earlier rare-earth magnet work.",
        "response": "The magnets make a strong, mature case, and their practical consequences are unmistakable. I give IceCube priority because it established a new means of observing the universe and a new field of astronomy; the magnets’ independent development also leaves a historical attribution question that deserves the same care."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "You find optical lattice clocks mature and their two-person attribution comparatively clear, while asking that their fundamental discovery be weighed against their precision-instrument achievement.",
        "response": "That clarity and maturity make B044 a strong alternative. I favor IceCube because its instrument enabled an entirely new observational channel and an established body of astrophysical discoveries. The clock’s exceptional precision is consequential, but the balance between measurement achievement and physical discovery remains a central question for that case."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The individual contributions of the proposed IceCube laureates relative to other collaboration members need careful historical assessment.",
      "The eventual scientific reach of high-energy neutrino astronomy is still developing."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct profile-based connection to the proposed laureates is apparent; my profile describes broad astroparticle and detector expertise but no IceCube affiliation."
  }
}
